; The command line of the server binary, over the real binary: `--version`,
; `--help`, an option it does not take, and the `check` mode that was already
; there. One `.t` per behaviour.
;
; Every input is either a `.t` file, which dune adds to the stanza itself, or
; one of the four entries below: the binary and the three files a test reads
; to check what the binary says against where the fact comes from. A file a
; test reads that is not listed here would not invalidate its recorded
; result, so a dependency added later belongs in this list too.
;
; The binary is declared even though dune would build it anyway: the
; dependency is what makes a rebuilt server re-run these tests instead of
; leaving a recorded pass from the previous binary in place.
;
; None of the tests starts a protocol session: the point of the refusal test
; is that a bad command line never reaches the loop, so nothing here waits on
; a client, and no test needs `bounded.sh`.

(cram
 (deps
  %{exe:../../../lsp-server/native/main.exe}
  ../../../controller/version.ml
  ../../../vendor/jasmin.lock))
